{"id": 6, "ts": 1779615525.4498785, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "Based on the evidence we have so far, Candidate\u202fB stands out for operational reliability (very conscientious) and stress resilience (handles stress very well), both critical for long\u2011distance flights. However, B\u2019s interpersonal style raises concerns \u2013 being perceived as pretentious and occasionally using an inappropriate tone, which could affect crew cooperation and professional communication.\nCandidate\u202fA shows strong technical insight (anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision) and leadership qualities, but the notes flag unfriendly behavior and unhealthy habits, which may impact crew cooperation and long\u2011term reliability.\nCandidate\u202fC demonstrates rapid, correct decision\u2011making and excellent attention to detail, yet struggles with communication, is seen as egocentric, and is not eager to pursue further education \u2013 all potential red flags for teamwork and adaptability.\nCandidate\u202fD displays solid problem\u2011solving, concentration, and a serious sense of responsibility, but we lack any direct observations on crew cooperation, communication style, or how his moody, loner, and arrogant traits might affect cockpit dynamics.\nTo fill the most critical gaps, could we get any interview observations on Candidate\u202fA\u2019s reliability (dependability, consistency) and stress resilience (calmness under pressure)? This will help us compare A more directly with B on the core safety criteria.", "thoughts": "We have just received answer from Agent 1 (Anna Keller) confirming no observations on D's crew cooperation, professional communication, mood/ loner. So we have that info.\n\nNow as Agent 3, need to contribute a public message. We should summarize current evidence, maybe compare candidates on a key criterion. Since we have limited info, maybe focus on reliability